{"t":1794091508,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"6c0fb610-5c10-4ed5-ad66-9ab2d35b8d9b","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-11-07T19:14:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 071914\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n214 PM EST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nA brisk afternoon continues, though winds will subside for most\nlower elevation locations this evening. A storm system may\napproach late next week.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Dry and seasonable this weekend.\n\n- 2) Dry and mild Monday through Wednesday.\n\n- 3) Colder and unsettled Thursday and Friday.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and seasonable this weekend.\n\nHigh pressure will build near and south of the region through\nthe weekend as an upper trough axis pulls away to the east.\nBrisk west to northwest winds will usher in cooler, more\nseasonable temperatures. Winds will gradually diminish this\nevening, except at higher elevations and over the waters. \nSunday will still be breezy, but about 5-10 mph lower.\n\nWave clouds/stratocu likely persist west of I-81 into the\nevening. It will be mainly clear and dry otherwise.\n\nThe central Virginia piedmont will be closer to the high and\nhave a chance to decouple (winds going light/calm) tonight and\nagain Sunday night. Near freezing temperatures are expected as a  \nresult. Frost/freeze headlines have been issued, and if current\nguidance is correct, the growing season may end tonight for\nseveral zones in central Virginia.\n \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry and mild Monday through Wednesday.\n\nOne dry front will cross by Monday, leading to breezy northwest\nwinds returning. Cold advection behind this front is modest, so\nhigh temperatures will likely be similar to Sunday when combined\nwith downsloping effects.\n\nAnother dry/weak front will move across the region Tuesday. \nTemperatures will be about 5 to 8 degrees above average as there\ncould be a warming surface wind out of the southwest. Highs \nwill reach the middle 60s to lower 70s with upper 50s to near 60\nin the mountains. Lows Tuesday night will drop into the upper \n30s to mid 40s. Once the front moves through, a surface high \npressure will quickly build into and across the region for \nWednesday. This will allow for temperatures to remain above \naverage by a few degrees and the day to be dry. Highs in the \nmiddle to upper 60s with middle 50s in the mountains. The high \nquickly moves east by Wednesday night. Lows mid 30s to mid 40s. \nThe colder temperatures could support some frost/freeze concerns\nwhere the growing season continues mainly east of the Blue Ridge.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Colder and unsettled Thursday and Friday.\n\nLooking into the Thursday and Friday timeframe, an area of low \npressure could move along the Ohio Valley or Lower Great Lakes and \npush into parts of the Mid-Atlantic region. An associated cold \nfront will trail the main low. Some rain showers could accompany\nthe low and front. During the same timeframe, a secondary low \npressure system (or multiple smaller low pressure systems) will\ntry to catch up to the first low and thus intensify it near the\nEastern Seaboard. There are some discrepancies with the many \nmodels in the position, timing, and intensity of the secondary \nlow pressure system. As for now, there is a higher confidence \nthat the weather could become unsettled with rain showers or \nperiods of rain, as well as some higher elevation snow or \nrain/snow mix. It is too early to tell where or to what extent \nany snow will evolve in any areas further to the east and \nsoutheast in our region. Highs Thursday only in the upper 50s to\nlower 60s and Friday will have highs in the upper 40s to middle\n50s with some mid/upper 30s for highs in the Appalachians.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /18Z SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nVFR conditions are expected at all TAF sites through the\nweekend. Few-sct stratocu around FL040-060 are possible \nespecially near MRB. Gusty W/NW winds will gradually diminish \nwith gusts becoming occasional tonight (perhaps notably lighter \nnear CHO). Winds take a bit more W/SW component Sun, generally \n3-8 kt less than Sat. VFR persists Mon with renewed gusty W/NW\nwinds to around 25 kts.\n\nVFR conditions Tuesday and Wednesday. Winds southwest to west 5 to \n10 knots Tuesday. Winds west then light and variable Wednesday.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nGusty west to northwest winds are expected through this evening,\nsolidly into SCA thresholds with a few peak gusts around 30 kts \npossible at times. Winds gradually subside through Sunday while\ntaking on a bit more of a westerly component. A dry front will\nreinvigorate west to northwest wind gusts late Sunday night\nthrough Monday with additional SCAs likely.\n\nSmall Craft Advisories possible Tuesday for the upper and \nmiddle Chesapeake Bay. No marine hazards expected Wednesday. \nWinds southwest 10 to 15 knots gusts up to 20 knots possible\nTuesday. Winds becoming west 5 to 10 knots Wednesday.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...Freeze Warning from midnight tonight to 9 AM EST Sunday for \n     VAZ036>039-050-051-502.\n     Frost Advisory from midnight tonight to 9 AM EST Sunday for \n     VAZ040-055-056-501-526-527.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory until 6 AM EST Monday for ANZ530>534-\n     537>543.\n     Small Craft Advisory until 6 PM EST Sunday for ANZ535-536.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...KLW/DHOF\nAVIATION...KLW/DHOF\nMARINE...KLW/DHOF\n"},"forecast":{"t":1794091442,"days":{"2026-11-07":{"hi":60,"lo":43,"pop":1,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-11-08":{"hi":64,"lo":44,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-09":{"hi":64,"lo":40,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-10":{"hi":67,"lo":44,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-11":{"hi":67,"lo":44,"pop":6,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-12":{"hi":62,"lo":41,"pop":47,"shor